{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794017657,"nonce":"DkNTekftvf8AACg4P00lEV2j5iw=","title":"Text wraps one character per line at 3x display density (Calendar day numbers)","message":"Reported on an iPhone 12 Pro Max in Safari and Chrome: the cn Calendar's two-digit day numbers break into two lines (\"1\" over \"0\"). It is not iOS-specific. It reproduces in desktop Chrome at deviceScaleFactor 3 (WebGPU), and natively by rendering the calendar fixture at scale 3; at 1x and 2x it renders correctly. Older runtimes (before the 2026-11-06 text, IME and clippy commits) show it too, so it is long-standing.\n\nCause: a text box is sized by layout to a whole logical pixel, so it can be up to a pixel narrower than the text it holds. The renderer wraps when the box is more than 2 physical pixels narrower than the measured text (`effective_width \u003c measured_width - 2.0`), and that slack was fixed in physical pixels. For \"10\" at 13px the box is 14 logical and the text 14.87, a gap of 2.6 physical pixels at 3x, over the 2.0 allowance; at 2x it is 1.7, under it. Once the decision is made, the line breaker has only per-character break points, so each digit lands on its own line.\n\nFix: the allowance scales with the density (`2.0 * scale.max(1.0)`), at the four places that make the decision (frame, captured and composite-replay text). Checked: native render at 3x, desktop Chrome at dpr 3 with the rebuilt runtime, 1536 tests across blinc_app, blinc_layout, blinc_cn, blinc_text and blinc_gpu.\n\nNot addressed: the month and year selects in the Calendar header clip their label slightly at every density (\"Novembe\" / \"2026\"), which is a separate fixed-width trigger problem. The underlying mismatch (layout measures 14.0, the renderer 14.87) is unchanged; the allowance only stops it showing as a wrap.","files":null}]}